.SH NAME
flexdw \- list, extract, add and change files on FLEX and UniFLEX disc images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flexdw
.RI [ "global options" ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.IR arguments ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B flexdw
is the console version of FLEX Disk Wrangler: the same detection, the same checks and the same checked save as the
window, with no display needed, so it works in scripts.
It reads and writes discs of TSC's FLEX, in whatever geometry the disc's System Information Record gives, and of
UniFLEX, TSC's multi-user system for the same 6809 machines (8-inch floppies and Winchester hard discs), changing
them as FLEX and UniFLEX themselves do.
The discs may be raw images
.RI ( .dsk ,
as most emulators write them), ImageDisk
.RI ( .imd ),
DSK or extended DSK files, or TeleDisk
.RI ( .td0 )
files, which can only be read.
.PP
Global options may come before or after the command.
An image and a path on it are always separate arguments
.RB ( "flexdw get disc.dsk GAME.CMD out/" ),
never
.IR image : path .
.PP
Each run opens the image, makes its changes as one transaction and saves once: the disc is checked first, the new
file is written beside the old one and read back, then put in its place.
If anything fails, the image is left exactly as it was.
.SS Paths
A FLEX disc has one directory:
.B GAME.CMD
or
.BR /GAME.CMD .
A UniFLEX disc is a tree:
.BR /usr/bin/edit .
The wildcards
.B *
and
.B ?\&
expand in
.BR get ,
.BR rm ,
.B attr
and
.BR ls .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON document on standard output (see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" )
and nothing else there.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Do everything, including every check, but write nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-vol= NAME
Which volume of the image:
.B flex
or
.BR uniflex .
.B all
lists every volume, for
.B ls
and
.BR info .
.TP
.BI \-\-fs= ID
Read the image as this filesystem, whatever detection says
.RB ( info
lists the readings and their ids).
.TP
.B \-\-backup
Keep the old file as
.IB name .bak
when saving.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No output on success.
.TP
.BR \-f " \fINAME\fR, " \-\-format= \fINAME\fR
On
.BR new ,
the format to make (one of those
.B new \-\-list
gives).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the commands and global options;
.IB command " \-\-help"
prints that command's own options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and the DiskWranglerCore version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS info
.B flexdw info
.I image
.PP
What the image is: the container, the detection result and the other readings, and the volume with its name and
free space.
.SS check
.B flexdw check
.I image
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.PP
Check the disc's whole filesystem.
Exit status 4 if it fails;
.B \-\-strict
counts warnings as failures too.
.SS ls
.B flexdw ls
.I image
.RI [ path ]
.RB [ \-R ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.PP
List a directory: name, type
.RB ( D
for a directory), attributes (a UniFLEX file's access:
.B rwx
for its owner,
.B RWX
for others), size and date.
A FLEX file's size is shown with a
.B ~
in front
.RB ( ~504 ):
FLEX keeps only whole sectors, so the true length isn't known.
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
lists every directory below the path;
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory
shows the path itself.
.SS get
.B flexdw get
.I image path... hostdir
.PP
Extract files and directories into the host folder
.IR hostdir ,
or, with
.B \-
as
.IR hostdir ,
write one file to standard output.
Names are lower-cased.
A FLEX file comes out in whole sectors, a random-access file without its sector map.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-manifest= FILE
Also write a retro-files manifest of what was extracted.
.TP
.BR \-\-replace ", " \-\-skip
What to do with a host file of the same name: replace it, or leave it and go on.
Without either, the run stops with exit status 5.
.RE
.SS put
.B flexdw put
.I image host... dir
.PP
Add host files and folders to
.I dir
.RB ( /
on a FLEX disc).
Names are made valid for the filesystem; a name that clashes within one
.B put
gets
.BR ~1 ,
.BR ~2 ...
A UniFLEX file gets the time it was written, as UniFLEX's
.B copy
gives it.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-replace ", " \-\-skip
What to do when a file of that name is there.
Without either, the run stops with exit status 5.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Accepted as the other Disk Wranglers accept it; a FLEX file that is write-, delete- or read-protected still can't be
replaced, as in FLEX: take the protection off with
.B attr
first.
.TP
.BI \-\-retro= FILE
Take names and attributes (a FLEX file's protection, a UniFLEX file's owner and access) from a retro-files manifest
or JSON file (the drag-and-drop format shared with Acorn Disk Wrangler and CP/M Disk Wrangler); may be given more
than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-letters= dirs | flatten
Accepted as the other Disk Wranglers accept it (Acorn DFS files arriving on an ADFS disc); it changes nothing here.
.RE
.SS rm
.B flexdw rm
.I image path...
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.PP
Delete files.
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
deletes a UniFLEX directory with everything in it.
A protected FLEX file can't be deleted, even with
.BR \-\-force .
.SS mkdir
.B flexdw mkdir
.I image path...
.RB [ \-p ]
.PP
Make UniFLEX directories;
.BR \-p ", " \-\-parents
makes the ones above too.
FLEX has none (exit status 6).
.SS mv
.B flexdw mv
.I image from to
.PP
Rename a file, or, on UniFLEX, move a file or directory to another directory (a directory's
.B ..\&
is rewritten).
.SS attr
.B flexdw attr
.I image path...
.RB [ \-\-set
.IR key = value ]...
.PP
Show a file's attributes, or change them with
.BR \-\-set ,
which may be given more than once.
On FLEX the protection:
.BR write ,
.BR delete ,
.B read
and
.B catalog
(shown:
.B random
and
.BR sectors ).
On UniFLEX:
.BR ownerRead ,
.BR ownerWrite ,
.BR ownerExecute ,
.BR othersRead ,
.BR othersWrite ,
.BR othersExecute ,
.B setUserId
and
.B owner
(shown:
.BR links ,
.B fdn
and
.BR type ).
Yes/no values are
.BR yes / no ,
.BR on / off ,
.BR true / false
or
.BR 1 / 0 .
An unknown key is refused with the list of the filesystem's keys.
.SS title
.B flexdw title
.I image
.RI [ text ]
.PP
Show or set the volume name (FLEX, up to 8 characters) or pack name (UniFLEX).
.SS boot
.B flexdw boot
.I image
.RI [ option ]
.PP
FLEX and UniFLEX discs have no boot option: this exists for the other Disk Wranglers and fails with exit status 6.
.SS new
.B flexdw new
.I file
.BI \-\-format= ID
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B flexdw new \-\-list
.PP
Make a blank disc as FLEX's NEWDISK lays one out, or a UniFLEX one as TSC's formatter does
.RB ( uniflex\-ss\-sd ,
.BR uniflex\-ss\-dd ,
.BR uniflex\-ds\-sd ,
.BR uniflex\-ds\-dd ).
A new UniFLEX disc has no swap space and no bootstrap: it holds files but doesn't start UniFLEX.
The disc is checked and opened again before it is kept.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the formats.
.TP
.BI \-\-title= TEXT
The volume name (FLEX) or pack name (UniFLEX).
.TP
.BI \-\-container= KIND
.B raw
(the default),
.B imd
(ImageDisk),
.B dsk
or
.BR edsk ,
where the format can be held in it.
An ImageDisk file has the sides, density and single-density track 0 TSC's format has.
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Replace the file if it exists.
.RE
.SS convert
.B flexdw convert
.I image
.RI [ out ]
.RB [ \-\-container= \fIKIND\fR]
.PP
Write the disc in another container
.RB ( raw ,
.BR imd ,
.B dsk
or
.BR edsk ),
to
.I out
or over the image.
TeleDisk files can't be written, so convert one to change it.
.B \-\-replace
replaces
.I out
if it exists.
.SS copy
.B flexdw copy
.I src dest path...
.RB [ \-\-into= \fIDIR\fR]
.RB [ \-\-to\-vol= \fINAME\fR]
.PP
Copy files from one image to another (or within one), mapping names and attributes as drag and drop does.
.B \-\-into
names the directory they go to,
.B \-\-to\-vol
the volume of
.IR dest .
.BR \-\-replace ,
.BR \-\-skip ,
.B \-\-force
and
.B \-\-letters
are as for
.BR put .
.SS script
.B flexdw script
.I image
.RI [ file | \- ]
.PP
Run many commands on one image with one save.
Each line of
.I file
(or standard input) is a command without
.B flexdw
and the image
.RB ( "put host/notes.txt /" );
lines starting
.B #
are comments.
Global options go on the
.B script
command itself;
.BR new ,
.B convert
and
.B script
can't be used in a script.
It stops at the first line that fails, and then nothing is saved.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
With
.BR \-\-json ,
standard output is one JSON document (schema 1):
.BR schema ,
.BR tool ,
.BR command ,
.BR image ,
.BR vol ,
.BR ok ,
.BR exit ,
.BR dryRun ,
.BR saved ,
.B result
(which depends on the command),
.B changes
(one per file added, deleted, renamed or changed),
.B warnings
and
.B error
(null, or
.BR code ,
.BR kind ,
.B message
and
.BR path ).
Scripts should read
.B exit
for the outcome and the rest for detail.
Text output is for people and may change between versions; the JSON only gains fields.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 4
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed (the disc is full, a bad name, a protected file, a file that can't be read).
.TP
.B 2
Wrong arguments.
.TP
.B 3
The image, a path on it, or a host file isn't there.
.TP
.B 4
The disc fails its check, so nothing was written.
.TP
.B 5
A name exists and neither
.B \-\-replace
nor
.B \-\-skip
was given.
.TP
.B 6
Unsupported, or read-only: a TeleDisk file, something the filesystem can't do (a directory on FLEX).
.TP
.B 7
The disc belongs to another Disk Wrangler (an Acorn or CP/M disc;
.B info
names it).
.TP
.B 8
Two readings are equally likely: choose one with
.BR \-\-fs .
.TP
.B 9
Nothing recognised the disc
.RB ( \-\-fs
tries a reading anyway).
.TP
.B 10
The checked save failed; the original file is untouched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
If set, the time used for new files' dates and ImageDisk headers, so that a run gives the same bytes every time.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
# what the disc is
flexdw info DISC.DSK

# list it
flexdw ls DISC.DSK

# every file into the folder out
flexdw get DISC.DSK / out

# check the disc
flexdw check DISC.DSK

# add a file
flexdw put DISC.DSK notes.txt /

# see what a delete would do, without doing it
flexdw \-\-dry\-run rm DISC.DSK OLD.TXT

# a blank 40\-track FLEX disc called WORK
flexdw new NEW.DSK \-\-format=flex\-40x10 \-\-title=WORK

# a UniFLEX disc's whole tree
flexdw ls UNIFLEX.DSK \-R

# a directory on a UniFLEX disc
flexdw mkdir UNIFLEX.DSK /work

# a blank UniFLEX disc as ImageDisk
flexdw new NEW.IMD \-\-format=uniflex\-ds\-dd \-\-title=pack \-\-container=imd

# the listing as JSON, for a script
flexdw \-\-json ls DISC.DSK
.EE
